# stdlib.roaring Tutorial

`stdlib.roaring` implements Roaring Bitmaps: 32-bit integers are partitioned by their high 16 bits into hybrid containers — sorted `u16` arrays for sparse chunks (`<= 4096` elements) and 8 KiB bitmaps for dense chunks.

## Core API

- `new_bitmap() -> map`: Create an empty roaring bitmap
- `add(b: map, v: int) -> map`: Insert a 32-bit integer (automatically promotes array containers above 4096 entries)
- `has(b: map, v: int) -> bool`: Test set membership in `O(log n)` (array) or `O(1)` (bitmap)
- `cardinality(b: map) -> int`: Total element count across all containers
- `union(a: map, b: map) -> map`: Set union of two roaring bitmaps
- `to_list(b: map) -> list`: Materialize all elements in sorted order

## Runnable Example

See `examples/roaring_tutorial.ltl` and `tests/stdlib_roaring_tutorial.ltl`:

```lateralus
import roaring

let a = new_bitmap()
a = add(a, 1)
a = add(a, 42)
a = add(a, 65536)
a = add(a, 65537)
a = add(a, 131072)
a = add(a, 42)

let b = new_bitmap()
b = add(b, 42)
b = add(b, 999)
b = add(b, 65537)

let u = union(a, b)
assert(cardinality(a) == 5)
assert(has(a, 65536) == true)
assert(has(a, 65535) == false)
assert(cardinality(u) == 6)
assert(to_list(u) == [1, 42, 999, 65536, 65537, 131072])
```

## Pinned Test Vectors

| Operation | Expected Output |
|---|---|
| `cardinality(a)` after inserting `[1, 42, 65536, 65537, 131072, 42]` | `5` |
| `has(a, 65536)` / `has(a, 65535)` | `true` / `false` |
| `to_list(a)` | `[1, 42, 65536, 65537, 131072]` |
| `cardinality(union(a, b))` with `b = {42, 999, 65537}` | `6` |
| `to_list(union(a, b))` | `[1, 42, 999, 65536, 65537, 131072]` |
